Code Review: Bruce.Core Sprint 1
Executive Summary
The foundation is functional but has architectural gaps that will cause friction in Sprint 2+. Main concerns: tight coupling in BruceEngine, inconsistent error handling, missing validation, and some thread-safety holes.

1. BruceEngine.cs — Facade Pattern Issues
Problem: God object emerging
csharppublic class BruceEngine : IDisposable
{
    private readonly JsonStore _store;  // Single store handles 5 interfaces
The engine directly exposes store interfaces (Tasks, Workers, etc.) which bypasses the facade and lets consumers mutate state without triggering events or state machine checks.
Recommendation:

Don't expose raw stores publicly
All mutations go through engine methods
Consider CQRS split: IBruceCommands / IBruceQueries

Problem: Inconsistent ID generation
csharpId = Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N")[..12],  // Task
Id = Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N")[..8],   // Worker
12 chars for tasks, 8 for workers. No prefix. Collision probability is low but debugging will be harder.
Recommendation:
csharppublic static class IdGen
{
    public static string Task() => $"T-{Guid.NewGuid():N}"[..10];
    public static string Worker() => $"W-{Guid.NewGuid():N}"[..10];
    public static string Message() => $"M-{Guid.NewGuid():N}"[..10];
}
Problem: No validation
csharppublic BruceTask CreateTask(string title, string description, ...)
{
    // No null/empty checks
    // No title length limits
    // No description sanitization
Problem: Silent failures
csharppublic bool AssignTask(string taskId, string workerId)
{
    var task = _store.Get(taskId);
    var worker = ((IWorkerStore)_store).Get(workerId);
    if (task == null || worker == null) return false;  // Why did it fail?
Caller has no idea if task doesn't exist, worker doesn't exist, or capacity exceeded.
Recommendation: Result pattern
csharppublic record AssignResult(bool Success, string? Error = null);
// or
public record Result<T>(T? Value, string? Error);

2. JsonStore.cs — Persistence Concerns
Problem: Write amplification
csharppublic void Save(BruceTask task)
{
    lock (_lock)
    {
        _tasks[task.Id] = task;
        SaveFile("tasks.json", _tasks);  // Rewrites entire file on every save
    }
}
With 81 tasks max this is fine, but pattern doesn't scale. More importantly, crash during write = data loss.
Recommendation:

Write to temp file, then atomic rename
Or append-only log with periodic compaction (aligns with phext philosophy)

csharpprivate void SaveFileAtomic<T>(string filename, T data)
{
    var path = Path.Combine(_basePath, filename);
    var temp = path + ".tmp";
    var json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(data, JsonOpts);
    File.WriteAllText(temp, json);
    File.Move(temp, path, overwrite: true);
}
Problem: No serialization for BruceCoord
BruceCoord is a readonly struct. System.Text.Json won't serialize it properly without a converter.
csharppublic readonly struct BruceCoord  // No JsonConverter, will serialize as {}
Recommendation: Add converter or switch to record:
csharppublic record BruceCoord(int EntityType, int Major, int Minor);

3. Distributor.cs — Logic Gaps
Problem: Race condition in Pull
csharppublic BruceTask? Pull(Worker worker, TaskType? preferredType = null)
{
    var available = _tasks.GetByState(TaskState.Reviewed)
        .Where(t => t.AssignedTo == null)
        .ToList();
    // ... later ...
    return ClaimTask(task, worker);  // Task could be claimed by another worker between query and claim
}
Two workers calling Pull simultaneously could both see the same task as available.
Recommendation: Optimistic locking or claim-with-check:
csharpprivate BruceTask? ClaimTask(BruceTask task, Worker worker)
{
    // Re-fetch and verify still unassigned
    var current = _tasks.Get(task.Id);
    if (current == null || current.AssignedTo != null)
        return null;  // Already claimed
    // proceed...
}
Problem: Capacity check doesn't account for in-flight
If a worker has 2 adhoc slots and 2 tasks are being assigned simultaneously, both could pass CanAccept.

4. TaskStateMachine.cs — Missing Transitions
Problem: Can't unassign without rejecting
csharp[TaskState.Assigned] = new[] { TaskState.Testing, TaskState.Reviewed },
Going from Assigned → Reviewed clears the assignee. But what if you want to reassign to a different worker? Current flow:

Assigned → Reviewed (clears worker)
Reviewed → Assigned (new worker)

This loses the assignment history.
Recommendation: Add explicit Reassign operation or track assignment history separately.
Problem: State machine doesn't validate assignee exists
csharpif (newState == TaskState.Assigned && string.IsNullOrEmpty(task.AssignedTo))
    return false;
```

Checks if `AssignedTo` is set but not if that worker actually exists.

---

### 5. BruceCoord.cs — Address Space

**Problem: 81 slots per entity is tight**

9×9 = 81 tasks. For a team of 3 humans + 18 AI, each managing 4-24 tasks, you could hit this in a week.

**Current allocation:**
```
1.1.1/1.1.1/2.x.x  → Tasks (81 slots)
Recommendation: Either:

Expand to full L3 space: 1.1.1/1.1.x/2.x.x = 729 slots
Or add archival: completed tasks move to different coordinate range

Problem: No reclamation
When tasks complete, their coordinates aren't freed. Allocator only moves forward.

6. Models.cs — Design Issues
Problem: Mutable models with no change tracking
csharppublic class BruceTask
{
    public string Title { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    // Anyone can mutate, no events fired
}
UI could do task.Title = "new" and store never knows.
Recommendation: Either:

Make models immutable (records with with expressions)
Or add dirty tracking
Or accept it and document "always save after mutation"

Problem: Worker.ViewCoord unused
csharppublic BruceCoord? ViewCoord { get; set; }  // Current context snapshot location
This is defined but never set or used. Dead code or incomplete feature?

7. Interfaces.cs — API Surface
Problem: Store interfaces expose implementation details
csharpIEnumerable<BruceTask> Query(Func<BruceTask, bool> predicate);
This forces in-memory filtering. Won't work with a real database.
Recommendation: Specification pattern or explicit query methods:
csharpIEnumerable<BruceTask> GetByStateAndType(TaskState state, TaskType? type);

8. Event Bus — Missing Features
Problem: No async support
csharpvoid Subscribe<T>(Action<T> handler);
All handlers are sync. SignalR/Blazor will want async handlers.
Problem: No event ordering guarantees
Events fire in subscription order, but if handler A publishes another event mid-flight, ordering gets weird.
Problem: No dead letter / error visibility
csharpcatch (Exception ex)
{
    Console.Error.WriteLine($"[EventBus] Handler error...");  // Swallowed
}

9. Thread Safety Audit
ComponentThread Safe?NotesJsonStorePartialLock protects dict, but file I/O can still raceInMemoryEventBusYesSnapshot before invokeDistributorNoQuery-then-mutate raceBruceEngineNoMultiple operations aren't atomicCoordAllocatorNoNo locking on _next

10. Missing Pieces for Sprint 2

No DTOs — Models are passed directly to UI. Changes to internal model break UI.
No logging — Just Console.Error in event bus
No configuration — Data path hardcoded
No health check — Can't verify store is working
No metrics hooks — You said no metrics, but event counts would help debugging


Priority Fixes
P0 (Before Sprint 2):

Add JSON converter for BruceCoord (data won't persist correctly without it)
Atomic file writes (data loss risk)
Fix race in Distributor.Pull

P1 (Early Sprint 2):
4. Result types instead of bool returns
5. Input validation on CreateTask/CreateWorker
6. Lock CoordAllocator
P2 (Can defer):
7. Async event handlers
8. DTO layer
9. Expand address space

Want me to implement the P0 fixes now, or wait for your input after meetings?


