Sprint 1 Retro
--------------
Sprint 2 Direction: CLI Layer
After sitting with it, I'm choosing Option B.

The Reasoning
The fundamental bottleneck right now isn't persistence format or visual debugging - it's that your AI workers have no hands.
Claudex2, GPT, and Gemini exist in workers.json but they can't actually do anything with Bruce. They can't claim tasks, post messages, or submit artifacts. The moment a CLI exists, every substrate with shell access becomes a participant.
This is the ramp-up optimization: make Bruce operable by the entities who will actually use it.

What We Build Tomorrow
bruce worker list
bruce worker status <id>

bruce task list [--state <state>]
bruce task create "<title>" --type <adhoc|planned> [--description "<desc>"]
bruce task show <id>
bruce task claim <task-id> --worker <worker-id>
bruce task advance <id>

bruce message send <worker-id> "<content>"
bruce message list [--worker <id>]

bruce status                    # System summary
bruce context <worker-id>       # Worker's current context
That's the minimal grammar. One sprint.

Why This Opens More Doors

Immediate testability. Between our sessions, you can run bruce task create "Test task" --type adhoc and verify state changes in the JSON files. You can script sequences and watch for race conditions.
AI substrate integration. Any tool-using model can invoke shell commands. Claude Code, ChatGPT with code interpreter, your Grok instance - they all get Bruce access through the same interface.
Phext comes through the CLI, not under it. Sprint 3 can add:

   bruce phext export [--file <path>]
   bruce phext sync --file <path>
   bruce phext diff <file1> <file2>
This keeps phext as a coordination layer rather than coupling it to storage internals.

Protocol discovery happens organically. Once your AI workers can actually claim and complete tasks, you'll observe how they communicate - what context they need, what they produce, where handoffs break. That's empirical data for Option E (Multi-Substrate Protocol).


What We're Deferring

xUnit discovery fix (can inline during CLI work if time permits)
Blazor dashboard (Sprint 4+)
Phext-native persistence (Sprint 3, informed by CLI usage patterns)
Formal protocol spec (Sprint 3-4, after we see real coordination)


Sprint 2 Success Criteria

dotnet run --project src/Bruce.Cli -- task list works
Full CRUD for tasks via CLI
Worker claim/release cycle works
Message send/receive works
At least one AI substrate successfully executes a Bruce command during your testing

