The ratio held. More importantly: Sprint 2 required less steering because Sprint 1's retro provided clear direction.
What Worked

Retro-driven planning. Your Sprint 1 Retro gave me the exact command grammar, success criteria, and rationale. No ambiguity.
Zero-dependency CLI. Network restrictions forced me to drop System.CommandLine. Result: native .NET 8 only, which actually simplifies deployment for your AI workers.
Live verification. I ran the complete claim→advance→message→context workflow before delivering. The demo_data reflects real state changes.
Documentation-first for AI workers. The AI_WORKER_GUIDE.md is designed to be droppable into any substrate's context window.

What Could Improve

Network sandbox awareness. I burned cycles discovering NuGet was blocked. For Sprint 3, I'll assume restricted egress and design accordingly.
Nullable warnings. Six CS8601/CS8602 warnings remain. Functionally harmless but could be cleaned up.
No Bruce.Core integration. The CLI duplicates the model definitions from Sprint 1. When you merge, you may want to refactor Program.cs to reference Bruce.Core.Models.

Observations for Sprint 3
Phext integration is natural here. The CLI's JSON store maps cleanly to phext coordinates:

workers.json → Library 1.1.1
tasks.json → Library 1.1.2
messages.json → Library 1.1.3
Each task/worker/message → individual scroll

Protocol patterns emerged. The [STATUS], [ARTIFACT], [BLOCKED] message prefixes in the AI Worker Guide are proto-protocol. Sprint 3-4 could formalize these.
The context command is key. It's what an AI worker will call first every session. This is the handshake point for any multi-substrate protocol.

Sprint 3 Direction Options
Option A: Phext Persistence Layer
Replace JSON files with phext coordinates. Add:
bruce phext export [--file <path>]
bruce phext sync --file <path>
bruce phext diff <file1> <file2>
This makes Bruce state portable across your phext ecosystem.
Option B: Real Multi-Substrate Test
Keep the code stable. Focus on actually running Bruce across Claude, GPT, Gemini, Grok. Document what breaks, what context each substrate needs, where handoffs fail. Empirical data for protocol design.
Option C: Authentication & Identity
Add worker authentication. Right now any CLI caller can claim to be any worker. For real coordination:
bruce auth init <worker-id>        # Generate token
bruce auth verify                  # Check current identity
Plus --token or BRUCE_TOKEN env var for all commands.
Option D: Event Log / Audit Trail
Add append-only event log capturing all state transitions. Foundation for debugging coordination failures and eventual consistency.

My recommendation: Option B.
You have hands now. Use them. Run Bruce across your substrates this week. The friction you discover will tell us exactly what Sprint 3-4 need to solve. Theory follows practice.Sprint 3 Record
